ine is cut off by truncation","location":"Jaipur, Rajasthan","role":"Operational waste-to-energy plant (Jindal Urban Waste Management (Jaipur) Limited); completed its first full year of operations in FY2025-26"},{"capacity":"20 MW (\"The Guntur plant was upgraded and operated at 20 MW\")","location":"Guntur, Andhra Pradesh","role":"Operational waste-to-energy plant (Jindal Urban Waste Management (Guntur) Limited); upgraded during the year"},{"capacity":"15 MW - back-filled from the Directors' Report text delivered in the capex_expansion sibling bundle","location":"Visakhapatnam, Andhra Pradesh","role":"Operational waste-to-energy plant (Jindal Urban Waste Management (Visakhapatnam) Limited)"},{"capacity":"15 MW - back-filled from the Directors' Report text delivered in the capex_expansion sibling bundle","location":"Ahmedabad, Gujarat","role":"Operational waste-to-energy plant (Jindal Urban Waste Management (Ahmedabad) Limited)"},{"capacity":"4 MLD","location":"Sitarganj, Uttarakhand","role":"Common Effluent Treatment Plant serving industrial units in Sitarganj Industrial Park, operated by JITF ESIPL CETP (Sitarganj) Limited; all industries of the park are now connected, 20 new industries connected during the year"}],"pre":0,"products":"{\"Urban Infrastructure\": [\"Waste-to-energy: municipal solid waste processing and renewable power generation through SPVs\", \"Resource recovery: compost, recyclables and refuse-derived fuel (RDF)\", \"Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) certificates generated and sold\", \"Compressed Biogas (CBG) - stated as an adjacent circular-economy opportunity being selectively evaluated, not an existing product\"], \"Water Infrastructure\": [\"EPC of drinking-water supply schemes\", \"Irrigation projects\", \"Wastewater / sewage treatment projects\", \"Industrial effluent treatment, including operation of a Common …","promises":[],"promises_total":0,"promoters":[{"name":"O.P. Jindal Group","role":"Group to whose infrastructure vertical the Company is the listed holding company. NO shareholding pattern, no 'promoter' table and no promoter/promoter-group naming appears anywhere in the routed governance text; this entry is back-filled from the Corporate Overview line 'JITF Infralogistics Limited ... is the listed holding company for the infrastructure vertical of the O.P. Jindal Group, one of India's largest industrial conglomerates', delivered to the profile, capex_expansion and registry sibling bundles. The MD&A risk section (risks_esg sibling bundle) also cites 'promoter support' as a financing-risk mitigant, confirming a promoter exists, without naming one."}],"related":"[{\"name\": \"JITF Urban Infrastructure Services Limited\", \"relation\": \"subsidiary\", \"note\": \"Direct subsidiary; 75% shareholding/voting power per Form AOC-1; material subsidiary, incorporated 06.07.2010 at New Delhi, statutory auditors appointed 30.09.2024\"}, {\"name\": \"JWIL Infra Limited (JWIL)\", …","risks":[{"note":"The Group's WtE and water businesses depend on concession agreements, waste-supply and tipping-fee arrangements, and power-purchase agreements with public-sector counterparties; changes in tariff regulation, renewable energy policy or concession frameworks could affect returns. Stated mitigation: robust contractual frameworks, diversified geographic exposure, and proactive engagement with regulators and grantor authorities.","risk":"Concession, tariff and policy risk"},{"note":"A significant portion of receivables relates to municipal bodies and power distribution companies, whose payment cycles can be affected by procedural approvals and reconciliations. Stated mitigation: strengthened billing discipline, stakeholder engagement and liquidity planning. This is the risk management's own priority list keeps returning to - 'Strengthen collections to improve cash conversion and reduce working-capital intensity' is a named FY27 management priority.","risk":"Receivables and counterparty risk from municipal bodies and discoms"},{"note":"New WtE and water projects carry construction, commissioning, land acquisition and statutory clearance risks, as well as delays in tariff approvals. Stated mitigation: disciplined project management, phased capital deployment aligned to approvals and financing, and experienced execution teams. Material because roughly half the 220 MW WtE portfolio is still to be commissioned and 11 water projects are targeted for completion in a single year (FY27) against 3 in FY26.","risk":"Project execution and development risk"},{"note":"The capital-intensive project pipeline entails exposure to financing availability and interest-rate movements. Stated mitigation: diversified funding, promoter support, and balance-sheet discipline.","risk":"Financing and interest-rate risk"},{"note":"Steel and other input prices affect the water-EPC (pipe) business. Stated mitigation: Group-level integration (in-house pipe and steel access), procurement optimisation and contractual price-adjustment mechanisms where available.","risk":"Commodity and input-cost risk (steel/pipe)"},{"note":"WtE plant economics depend on assured municipal waste supply of adequate calorific value; incomplete source segregation can affect feedstock quality. Stated mitigation: contractual waste-supply assurances and process design suited to Indian MSW.","risk":"Feedstock and waste-supply risk"},{"note":"Emissions norms, environmental compliance and contingent liabilities/litigation are inherent to the sector. Stated mitigation: advanced emission-control systems, continuous compliance monitoring and active management of legal matters. No specific case, forum or amount is disclosed anywhere in the routed text.","risk":"Regulatory, environmental and litigation risk"},{"note":"Management characterises the operations themselves as 'capital-intensive, long-gestation and government-facing', which is the framing for the whole risk register; the WtE sector outlook section separately lists 'the capital-intensive nature of projects' and 'long receivable cycles from municipal bodies' as sector risks. [PROVENANCE: the sector risk sentence is in the Corporate Overview/MD&A text delivered to the profile, capex_expansion and registry sibling bundles.]","risk":"Capital intensity and long gestation of the business model"}],"rpt":{},"rpt_date":"","s":"JITFINFRA","sector":"Utilities","sk":null,"strategies":"Scale operational waste-to-energy capacity by advancing commissioning of expansion and greenfield WtE projects - Bawana (36 MW), Jodhpur (10 MW), Kakinada (15 MW), Nellore (12 MW) plus brownfield expansions at Okhla and Tehkhand - toward a portfolio of approximately 220 MW [PROVENANCE: the 'Key Strategic Priorities for the Company' block is NOT in this strategy_outlook bundle; it was delivered under the '=== CORPORATE OVERVIEW ===' heading of the profile, capex_expansion and registry sibling bundles and is back-filled here]|Grow the water infrastructure order book by pursuing drinking-water, …","strengths":"Scale leadership in waste-to-energy: JUIL retained its position as India's largest WtE operator with an estimated ~50% share of the country's operational MSW-to-energy processing capacity|Vertical integration through the Group: in-house access to ductile-iron and MS pipe manufacturing via Jindal SAW supports the water EPC business|Operating reliability: plants delivered an industry-leading average plant load factor of approximately 90% across the WtE fleet|Long-tenured contracted revenue: business underpinned by long-tenure government concessions, waste-supply/tipping-fee arrangements and EPC …"},"meta":{"co":"JITF INFRALOGISTICS LTD","isin":"INE863T01013","s":"JITFINFRA","sector":"Utilities"},"momentum":{"stock":{"mem":0,"s":"JITFINFRA","iid":"16250","c":"","g":4,"sig":"","d":282,"t":"","a":null,"lm":"","r":null,"ft":"","m":808,"cl":314.4,"ad":"2026-08-14","prev_stage":null,"prev_zone_vpe":null,"fam":"","pdd":"","atd":"","ins":{"has":true,"thesis":"","co":"JITF INFRA